<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.17.1: Quadratic Expressions in Different Forms</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02045</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.17.1: Quadratic Expressions in Different Forms</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3b99879e-a145-4f87-b40c-cde4ae0c3d5f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up</title>

<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/05c4d743874e6414e9d720787af98245ec2791ad"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Here are graphs representing the functions \(f\) and \(g\) given by \(f(x)=x(x+6)\) and \(g(x)=x(x+6)+4\).</para>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Which graph represents \(f(x)=x(x+6)\)? How do you know?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>The lower graph (which goes through the origin) is the graph of \(f\). </para>
  <list>
    <item> The value of \(g\) is always 4 greater than the value of \(f\), no matter which value of \(x\) we choose. </item>
    <item> Rewriting the equation in standard form gives \(f(x)=x^2+6x\). This form shows that the \(y\)-intercept of the graph representing \(f\) is \((0,0)\). </item>
    <item> The expression that defines function \(f\) is in factored form. It shows that the two \(x\)-intercepts are \((0,0)\) and \((-6,0)\). </item>
  </list>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Which graph represents \(g(x)=x(x+6)+4\)? How do you know?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>The lower graph (which goes through the origin) is the graph of \(f\) and the upper one is the graph of \(g\).  </para>
  <list>
    <item> The value of \(g\) is always 4 greater than the value of \(f\), no matter which value of \(x\) we choose. </item>
    <item> Rewriting the equation in standard form gives \(g(x)=x^2+6x+4\).This form shows that the \(y\)-intercept of the graph representing \(g\) is \((0,4)\). </item>
  </list>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Where does the graph of \(f\) meet the \(x\)-axis? Explain how you know.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(x=-6\) and \(x=0\), because these are the two values of \(x\) that make \(x(x+6)\)  equal to 0.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
